# Instrumentation and Biomechanics
#
# How instruments read the body without loading it, how small channels make flow orderly, how tissues creep and relax, and how blood flow and wall shear reach the cells that line arteries.

#>bioengineering_instrumentation
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!bioengineering
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Instrumentation and Biomechanics"
 ~#claim: "Good biomedical instruments subtract interference the body shares with both electrodes, and the endothelium reads the pattern of blood flow through a PECAM-1 sensor that drives inflammation where flow is disturbed."
 ~#proof: "Webster for amplifiers and biosensors, Purcell for low Reynolds number flow, Fung for tissue mechanics, Womersley and Westerhof for pulsatile flow, Malek, Tzima, and Harry for shear sensing."
 ~#door: "Read the amplifier claims, then the microfluidics claims, then follow the shear claims to the corrected PECAM-1 entry."
 ~#audience: "advanced engineering and biology students"
 continuity: "Instrumentation and Biomechanics | where flow is disturbed"
 cold_open: "The cells lining an artery feel the pattern of blood flow, and a single sensor drives inflammation where flow is disturbed."
}

